1
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:07,030
Ross, Mon, is it okay if I bring someone to your parents' anniversary party?

2
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:08,320
Sure.

3
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:08,320
Yeah.

4
00:00:08,490 --> 00:00:11,790
Who's the guy?

5
00:00:08,490 --> 00:00:11,790
Parker, I met him at the dry cleaners.

6
00:00:12,370 --> 00:00:15,170
Did he put a little starch in your bloomers?

7
00:00:17,790 --> 00:00:19,790
Who said that?

8
00:00:20,130 --> 00:00:25,050
No, he's really great, though. He has this incredible zest for life.

9
00:00:25,340 --> 00:00:27,010
And he treats me like a queen.

10
00:00:27,180 --> 00:00:30,140
Except at night, when he treats me like the naughty girl I am.

11
00:00:32,310 --> 00:00:35,190
Oh, by the way, would it be okay if I give the toast this year?

12
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:38,310
Are you sure you want to after what happened at their 20th?

13
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:43,030
Yeah, I'd really like to.

14
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:43,030
Hopefully this time Mom won't boo you.

15
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:48,660
Yes. Every year Ross makes the toast, and it always makes them cry.

16
00:00:48,870 --> 00:00:54,540
This year, I'm gonna make them cry.

17
00:00:48,870 --> 00:00:54,540
You wonder why Ross is their favorite?

18
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:59,880
No, really, any time Ross makes a toast, everyone cries and pats him on the back.

19
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:04,800
And they all come up to me and say, "God, your brother."

20
00:01:05,050 --> 00:01:09,390
You know what they're gonna say this year? "God, you."

21
00:01:10,890 --> 00:01:13,890
Well, I can promise you, at least one person will be crying.

22
00:01:14,060 --> 00:01:18,310
I'm an actor, and any actor worth his salt can cry on cue.

23
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:22,570
Really? You can do that?

24
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:22,570
Oh, you kidding me? Watch.

25
00:01:32,450 --> 00:01:35,910
Well, I can't do it with you guys watching me.

26
00:02:16,410 --> 00:02:19,790
What are you doing?

27
00:02:16,410 --> 00:02:19,790
I'm working on my toast for the party.

28
00:02:19,960 --> 00:02:23,460
Or as I like to call it: "Sob-fest 2002."

29
00:02:24,670 --> 00:02:26,880
Hey, check this out.

30
00:02:24,670 --> 00:02:26,880
It's a dog.

31
00:02:27,090 --> 00:02:29,630
It's a dead dog.

32
00:02:29,880 --> 00:02:32,800
That's Chi-Chi. She died when I was in high school.

33
00:02:33,010 --> 00:02:36,930
You're gonna talk about their dead pet?

34
00:02:33,010 --> 00:02:36,930
It's good stuff, huh?

35
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:40,640
Hi.

36
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:40,640
Hey.

37
00:02:40,890 --> 00:02:43,980
Joey? You got a present for my parents? That's so sweet.

38
00:02:44,150 --> 00:02:48,070
Yeah. In honor of their 35th anniversary, I had a star named after them.

39
00:02:49,490 --> 00:02:55,410
That is so cool.

40
00:02:49,490 --> 00:02:55,410
And I got them Kama Sutra for the Elderly.

41
00:02:56,120 --> 00:02:58,950
Do you guys have any extra ribbon?

42
00:02:56,120 --> 00:02:58,950
Oh, yeah, sure.

43
00:02:59,160 --> 00:03:05,670
What do you need? We got, uh, lace, satin, sateen, raffia, gingham, felt...

44
00:03:06,670 --> 00:03:09,550
And I think my testicles may be in here too.

45
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:17,850
AW. Chi-Chi. Oh, I loved this dog.

46
00:03:18,010 --> 00:03:22,640
You know, uh, Monica couldn't get braces because Chi-Chi needed knee surgery.

47
00:03:23,810 --> 00:03:28,860
What?

48
00:03:23,810 --> 00:03:28,860
You were the 200-pounder who rode her.

49
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:36,030
Hey. Everybody, this is Parker. Parker, this is...

50
00:03:36,320 --> 00:03:41,250
No, wait, don't tell me. Let me guess: Joey, Monica, Ross, Rachel...

51
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:43,500
...and I'm sorry, Phoebe didn't mention you.

52
00:03:45,830 --> 00:03:49,250
Chandler, I'm kidding. Already you're my favorite.

53
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:54,380
Tell me a little about yourselves.

54
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:54,380
Uh, actually, we should get going.

55
00:03:56,340 --> 00:03:57,760
Classic Ross.

56
00:04:00,390 --> 00:04:03,890
Rachel, Rachel, look how you glow. May I?

57
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:06,480
I think you already are.

58
00:04:06,690 --> 00:04:10,110
Rachel, you have life growing inside you.

59
00:04:10,570 --> 00:04:16,030
Is there anything more miraculous... Ooh, a picture of a dog. Whose is this?

60
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:20,740
That's my old dog. He passed away years ago.

61
00:04:20,990 --> 00:04:27,080
Well, at least you were lucky to have him. Bow-wow, old friend. Bow-wow.

62
00:04:29,250 --> 00:04:32,590
So where's the party?

63
00:04:29,250 --> 00:04:32,590
Out on the island. It's in Massapequa.

64
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:38,550
Massapequa, it sounds magical. Is it steeped in Native American history?

65
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:40,970
Well, there is an Arby's in the shape of a tepee.

66
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:45,230
I've got my notes. You got the presents?

67
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:45,230
Yeah.

68
00:04:45,440 --> 00:04:47,270
I've got the car keys.

69
00:04:45,440 --> 00:04:47,270
We're driving?

70
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:49,440
Yeah.

71
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:49,440
Aces.

72
00:04:52,230 --> 00:04:56,400
So, uh, he seems like a nice guy.

73
00:04:52,230 --> 00:04:56,400
Yeah. Yeah, I like him a lot.

74
00:04:56,650 --> 00:05:01,580
You want to hang back and take a cab?

75
00:04:56,650 --> 00:05:01,580
Okay, otherwise I'm not going.

76
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:13,460
Hey, Mom.

77
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:13,460
This is such a great party.

78
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:18,800
Thirty-five years. Very impressive. Do you guys have any pearls of wisdom?

79
00:05:19,300 --> 00:05:23,560
Jack?

80
00:05:19,300 --> 00:05:23,560
Why serve food on such a sharp stick?

81
00:05:24,310 --> 00:05:27,810
It's a good question, Dad. It's a good question.

82
00:05:29,560 --> 00:05:33,940
Congratulations, you two.

83
00:05:29,560 --> 00:05:33,940
Thank you, we're so excited.

84
00:05:34,150 --> 00:05:36,570
And also, congratulations on your wedding.

85
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:38,570
What?

86
00:05:38,740 --> 00:05:42,660
Can we talk to you for just a moment?

87
00:05:38,740 --> 00:05:42,660
Yeah.

88
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:45,950
It's just a little thing.

89
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:50,830
While we think it's simply marvelous that you're having this baby out of wedlock...

90
00:05:52,630 --> 00:05:55,510
...some of our friends are less open-minded.

91
00:05:55,710 --> 00:05:58,300
Which is why we told them you're married.

92
00:05:55,710 --> 00:05:58,300
What?

93
00:05:58,510 --> 00:06:01,010
Thanks for going along with this.

94
00:06:02,090 --> 00:06:06,520
We have to pretend that we're married?

95
00:06:02,090 --> 00:06:06,520
Son, I had to shave my ears for tonight.

96
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:08,020
You can do this.

97
00:06:09,730 --> 00:06:11,850
Can you believe that?

98
00:06:09,730 --> 00:06:11,850
I know.

99
00:06:12,150 --> 00:06:16,480
If you're gonna do the ears, you may as well take a pass at the nose area.

100
00:06:16,690 --> 00:06:19,990
No, no, I don't want to have to lie about us being married.

101
00:06:20,200 --> 00:06:24,120
I know. I don't either. But it's their party, and it's one night.

102
00:06:24,330 --> 00:06:26,870
We don't even have to lie. We just won't say anything.

103
00:06:27,120 --> 00:06:30,410
If it comes up, we'll smile, we'll nod along...

104
00:06:30,670 --> 00:06:31,710
Ross?

105
00:06:30,670 --> 00:06:31,710
Rachel?

106
00:06:31,870 --> 00:06:35,210
Hi, Aunt Lisa. Uncle Dan.

107
00:06:35,380 --> 00:06:40,340
Congratulations on the baby and on the wedding.

108
00:06:41,720 --> 00:06:46,220
Here's something to get you started.

109
00:06:41,720 --> 00:06:46,220
So how's marriage treating you?

110
00:06:46,850 --> 00:06:50,020
Unbelievable. It's great.

111
00:06:46,850 --> 00:06:50,020
I love marriage.

112
00:06:50,230 --> 00:06:51,640
We'll see you later.

113
00:06:51,850 --> 00:06:54,060
Hey.

114
00:06:51,850 --> 00:06:54,060
Hi.

115
00:06:54,900 --> 00:06:57,900
What a beautiful place. What a great night.

116
00:06:58,150 --> 00:07:02,410
I have to tell you, being here with all of you in Event Room C...

117
00:07:04,410 --> 00:07:08,790
...I feel so lucky. To think of all the good times that have happened here.

118
00:07:08,950 --> 00:07:14,080
The birthdays, the proms, the mitzvahs, both bar and bat.

119
00:07:15,080 --> 00:07:19,260
But none of it will compare with tonight. My God, I don't want to forget this moment.

120
00:07:19,420 --> 00:07:23,630
It's like I want to take a mental picture of you all. Click.

121
00:07:25,470 --> 00:07:28,600
I don't think the flash went off.

122
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:34,560
I'm gonna go find the men's room.

123
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:34,560
I'll go with you.

124
00:07:34,730 --> 00:07:37,150
Somewhere, there is someone with a tranquilizer gun...

125
00:07:37,320 --> 00:07:40,070
...and a huge butterfly net looking for that man.

126
00:07:41,610 --> 00:07:46,450
I have to go too, but I don't want him complimenting my thing.

127
00:07:46,700 --> 00:07:49,490
I'm so glad we weren't in the car. Did he ever let up?

128
00:07:49,740 --> 00:07:54,000
He called the Long Island Expressway a concrete miracle.

129
00:07:54,580 --> 00:07:57,460
This room. This night.

130
00:07:57,750 --> 00:08:03,630
That waiter. His shoes. I must take a mental picture.

131
00:08:03,930 --> 00:08:05,470
Sorry.

132
00:08:09,140 --> 00:08:11,430
Were you guys making fun of Parker?

133
00:08:12,810 --> 00:08:15,600
That depends, how much did you hear?

134
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:20,270
Well, he's a little enthusiastic. What's wrong with that?

135
00:08:20,440 --> 00:08:24,320
It's just... It's so much.

136
00:08:20,440 --> 00:08:24,320
Well, so what? I like him.

137
00:08:24,490 --> 00:08:28,660
Do I make fun of the people you've dated? Tag, Janice, Mona.

138
00:08:28,950 --> 00:08:33,290
No, because friends don't do that. But do you want my opinion?

139
00:08:33,450 --> 00:08:38,790
In my opinion, your collective dating record reads like a who's who of human crap.

140
00:08:41,460 --> 00:08:44,760
I feel terrible.

141
00:08:41,460 --> 00:08:44,760
I know.

142
00:08:45,840 --> 00:08:47,760
What was wrong with Mona?

143
00:08:53,350 --> 00:08:56,020
Open it, open it, open it.

144
00:08:53,350 --> 00:08:56,020
Yeah, baby.

145
00:08:56,270 --> 00:09:00,150
So we never got to hear about your wedding.

146
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:05,280
We were surprised we weren't invited.

147
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:05,280
Oh, no, it was just our parents.

148
00:09:05,440 --> 00:09:06,530
It was a small wedding.

149
00:09:06,700 --> 00:09:11,370
But it was beautiful. It was small, but kind of spectacular.

150
00:09:11,530 --> 00:09:16,370
Where did you have it?

151
00:09:11,530 --> 00:09:16,370
On a cliff in Barbados, at sunset.

152
00:09:16,620 --> 00:09:20,710
And Stevie Wonder sang "isn't She Lovely" as I walked down the aisle.

153
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:21,880
Really?

154
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:21,880
Yeah.

155
00:09:22,040 --> 00:09:26,050
Stevie's an old family friend.

156
00:09:22,040 --> 00:09:26,050
Oh, my God. That sounds amazing.

157
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:30,050
I'd love to see pictures.

158
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:30,050
Yeah, so would I.

159
00:09:30,300 --> 00:09:35,850
You wouldn't think that Annie Leibovitz would forget to put film in the camera.

160
00:09:37,230 --> 00:09:39,690
Would you excuse us for a second?

161
00:09:42,570 --> 00:09:45,400
Um, what are you doing?

162
00:09:42,570 --> 00:09:45,400
What? I'm not you.

163
00:09:45,570 --> 00:09:47,400
This may be the only wedding leverhave.

164
00:09:49,660 --> 00:09:53,530
And I want it to be amazing.

165
00:09:49,660 --> 00:09:53,530
Okay. Okay. Ooh. Ooh.

166
00:09:53,700 --> 00:09:55,500
Maybe I rode in on a Harley.

167
00:09:55,700 --> 00:09:58,000
Okay, Ross, it has to be realistic.

168
00:10:03,040 --> 00:10:07,170
Say, uh, ahem, are you okay? You seem kind of quiet.

169
00:10:07,380 --> 00:10:11,010
No, I'm fine. I'm great. I'm with you.

170
00:10:07,380 --> 00:10:11,010
And I'm with you.

171
00:10:11,180 --> 00:10:13,100
What a great time to be alive.

172
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:17,560
Look at this plate bouncy thing.

173
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:21,190
What an inspired solution to man's plate-dispensing problems.

174
00:10:21,350 --> 00:10:22,690
Mm-hm. Yeah.

175
00:10:22,860 --> 00:10:26,860
Ah. Oysters. Let me feed you one.

176
00:10:22,860 --> 00:10:26,860
That's not necessary.

177
00:10:27,070 --> 00:10:28,570
Please?

178
00:10:27,070 --> 00:10:28,570
No, actually, I don't eat...

179
00:10:28,780 --> 00:10:32,110
I won't quit until you try one.

180
00:10:28,780 --> 00:10:32,110
Okay, fine, fine.

181
00:10:33,450 --> 00:10:37,870
Mm. Mm-hm. Good.

182
00:10:33,450 --> 00:10:37,870
What're they like? I've never had one.

183
00:10:39,870 --> 00:10:42,210
Why don't you just try one?

184
00:10:39,870 --> 00:10:42,210
Nah, they look too weird.

185
00:10:45,590 --> 00:10:48,010
What are you doing?

186
00:10:45,590 --> 00:10:48,010
Just going over my toast.

187
00:10:48,170 --> 00:10:50,340
Ah, those two will never know what hit them.

188
00:10:50,510 --> 00:10:55,640
I can't wait. They're gonna cry so hard, they'll be fighting for breath.

189
00:10:56,010 --> 00:10:59,730
You know, if you want to, I could just hold them down and you could just:

190
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:06,650
And my veil was lace, made by blind Belgian nuns.

191
00:11:06,900 --> 00:11:10,320
Blind?

192
00:11:06,900 --> 00:11:10,320
Well, not at first, but it was intricate work.

193
00:11:10,610 --> 00:11:14,820
And they said even though they lost their sight, it was all worth it.

194
00:11:15,280 --> 00:11:18,660
I bet you looked beautiful.

195
00:11:15,280 --> 00:11:18,660
Oh, well, I don't know about that.

196
00:11:18,910 --> 00:11:22,500
But there were some people that said I looked like a floating angel.

197
00:11:24,290 --> 00:11:28,920
How did you propose?

198
00:11:24,290 --> 00:11:28,920
Oh, yeah, that's a great story.

199
00:11:30,010 --> 00:11:34,180
Well, um, actually, I took her to the planetarium.

200
00:11:34,590 --> 00:11:37,350
That's where we had our first date.

201
00:11:37,510 --> 00:11:43,190
Um, she walked in, and I had the room filled with, uh, lilies, her favorite flower.

202
00:11:43,850 --> 00:11:47,650
That is so sweet.

203
00:11:43,850 --> 00:11:47,650
Shh. I want to hear the rest.

204
00:11:49,190 --> 00:11:52,860
Then Fred Astaire singing, uh, "The Way You Look Tonight"...

205
00:11:53,030 --> 00:11:55,950
...came on the sound system and the lights came down...

206
00:11:56,120 --> 00:11:57,280
...and I got on one knee...

207
00:11:57,530 --> 00:12:01,870
...and written across the dome, in the stars, were the words:

208
00:12:02,120 --> 00:12:03,370
"Will you marry me?"

209
00:12:05,670 --> 00:12:09,710
And the ring was the size of my fist.

210
00:12:11,090 --> 00:12:14,340
Hey, uh, Phoebe? Look, uh...

211
00:12:15,890 --> 00:12:19,560
...I want to apologize about before, okay? We were being jerks.

212
00:12:19,810 --> 00:12:22,430
Parker's a nice guy, and I'd like to get to know him.

213
00:12:22,600 --> 00:12:23,850
Then you better do it now.

214
00:12:24,060 --> 00:12:26,520
Why?

215
00:12:24,060 --> 00:12:26,520
Because I'm gonna kill him.

216
00:12:26,730 --> 00:12:28,440
What?

217
00:12:26,730 --> 00:12:28,440
You guys were right. He's just too excited about everything.

218
00:12:31,650 --> 00:12:36,030
I mean, I'm all for living life, but this is the Gellers' 35th anniversary.

219
00:12:36,240 --> 00:12:38,700
Let's call a spade a spade. This party stinks.

220
00:12:39,410 --> 00:12:41,410
I know. I'm having the worst time.

221
00:12:41,580 --> 00:12:45,750
There was a 15-minute line for the buffet, and when I finally got up to the plates...

222
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:49,170
...I slipped on a giant booger.

223
00:12:51,250 --> 00:12:54,420
Are you sure it wasn't an oyster?

224
00:12:55,260 --> 00:12:58,140
I guess it could've been. I didn't really look at it.

225
00:12:58,390 --> 00:13:02,350
I just wiped it on Chandler's coat and got the hell out of there.

226
00:13:02,600 --> 00:13:06,520
I just thought he was such a great guy. I was so excited about him.

227
00:13:06,730 --> 00:13:10,150
Well, hey, you should be excited about him.

228
00:13:10,400 --> 00:13:12,730
There's nothing wrong with him. He's a good guy.

229
00:13:12,940 --> 00:13:14,940
You think?

230
00:13:12,940 --> 00:13:14,940
Yeah, know what I think?

231
00:13:15,150 --> 00:13:18,910
We were all just being too negative.

232
00:13:15,150 --> 00:13:18,910
You're right.

233
00:13:19,160 --> 00:13:24,200
You're right. He's just embracing life. We could all stand to be more like Parker.

234
00:13:24,370 --> 00:13:27,960
You know what? I am like him. I'm a sunny, positive person.

235
00:13:28,420 --> 00:13:31,460
Actually, you have a little bit of an edge.

236
00:13:28,420 --> 00:13:31,460
What's that now?

237
00:13:33,590 --> 00:13:36,300
Oh, it's Parker.

238
00:13:33,590 --> 00:13:36,300
Look, the Bunny Hop.

239
00:13:36,590 --> 00:13:38,220
Oh, I love it.

240
00:13:36,590 --> 00:13:38,220
You do?

241
00:13:38,430 --> 00:13:43,140
Are you kidding? People acting like animals to music? Come on.

242
00:13:45,060 --> 00:13:47,770
Okay, it's time for the toast.

243
00:13:47,940 --> 00:13:51,940
I know that normally Ross gives the toast, but this year I'm gonna do it.

244
00:13:54,110 --> 00:13:57,650
No, it's gonna be great. Really.

245
00:13:58,490 --> 00:13:59,910
Okay, um...

246
00:14:00,160 --> 00:14:04,490
Mom, Dad, when I got married...

247
00:14:04,740 --> 00:14:08,160
...one of the things that made me sure that I could do it...

248
00:14:08,330 --> 00:14:10,790
...was the amazing example the two of you set for me.

249
00:14:11,420 --> 00:14:15,090
For that, and for so many other things, I wanna say thank you.

250
00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:18,880
I know I probably don't say it enough...

251
00:14:19,720 --> 00:14:21,760
...but I love you.

252
00:14:32,650 --> 00:14:35,190
When I look around this room...

253
00:14:35,360 --> 00:14:39,240
...I'm saddened by the thought of those who could not be with us.

254
00:14:39,530 --> 00:14:43,780
Nana, my beloved grandmother, who would so want to be here.

255
00:14:45,200 --> 00:14:47,040
But she can't...

256
00:14:47,290 --> 00:14:48,870
...because she's dead.

257
00:14:52,710 --> 00:14:55,630
As is our dog, Chi-Chi.

258
00:14:57,670 --> 00:15:00,670
I mean, look how cute she is... Was.

259
00:15:03,260 --> 00:15:05,850
Do me a favor, pass this to my parents.

260
00:15:06,060 --> 00:15:11,140
Remember, um, she's dead, okay? Her and Nana, gone.

261
00:15:21,700 --> 00:15:23,570
Hey, does anybody remember when...

262
00:15:23,860 --> 00:15:29,410
...Debra Winger had to say goodbye to her children in Terms of Endearment?

263
00:15:30,870 --> 00:15:32,370
Didn't see that?

264
00:15:33,290 --> 00:15:35,830
No movie fans?

265
00:15:37,590 --> 00:15:39,590
You want to hear something sad?

266
00:15:39,760 --> 00:15:41,590
Um, the other day I was watching 60 Minutes...

267
00:15:41,760 --> 00:15:44,260
...and there was this piece on these orphans in Romania...

268
00:15:44,510 --> 00:15:48,310
...who have been so neglected that they were incapable of love.

269
00:15:54,940 --> 00:15:58,320
You people are made of stone.

270
00:15:58,520 --> 00:16:00,780
Here's to Mom and Dad. Whatever.

271
00:16:08,200 --> 00:16:11,700
Thank you, Monica. That was, uh, interesting.

272
00:16:11,950 --> 00:16:15,920
Wasn't it interesting, Jack?

273
00:16:11,950 --> 00:16:15,920
Why don't I remember this dog?

274
00:16:19,290 --> 00:16:22,210
Ross? Why don't you give us your toast now?

275
00:16:22,420 --> 00:16:25,510
Oh, no, Mom. It's just Monica this year.

276
00:16:26,550 --> 00:16:30,930
You're not gonna say anything? On our 35th wedding anniversary?

277
00:16:32,390 --> 00:16:34,140
No, of course, um...

278
00:16:36,350 --> 00:16:41,900
Um, everybody? Um, ahem, I just wanted to say...

279
00:16:42,070 --> 00:16:45,950
...uh, on behalf of my new bride, Rachel...

280
00:16:49,240 --> 00:16:53,160
...and myself, um, that if...

281
00:16:53,870 --> 00:16:59,330
If in 35 years, we're half as happy as you guys are...

282
00:16:59,590 --> 00:17:02,630
...we'll count ourselves the luckiest people in the world.

283
00:17:04,300 --> 00:17:06,260
Oh, Ross.

284
00:17:09,300 --> 00:17:13,930
I just wish Nana were alive to hear Ross' toast.

285
00:17:20,520 --> 00:17:23,940
My God, what a fantastically well-lit hallway.

286
00:17:25,400 --> 00:17:29,620
Can I, um, get you something to drink, like a water and Valium?

287
00:17:30,780 --> 00:17:35,000
I must say, this apartment, it's...there are no words.

288
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:37,830
Oh, thank God.

289
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:37,830
It's a haven.

290
00:17:38,040 --> 00:17:43,380
A third-floor paradise, a modern-day Eden.

291
00:17:38,040 --> 00:17:43,380
Yeah, I know, I know. Uh-huh. Gotcha.

292
00:17:43,550 --> 00:17:46,510
Listen, why don't we just, um, sit and relax,

293
00:17:46,750 --> 00:17:50,010
you know, just be with each other quietly.

294
00:17:50,640 --> 00:17:52,550
That sounds great.

295
00:17:53,390 --> 00:17:57,310
My God, this is the most comfortable couch I've ever sat on in my entire life.

296
00:17:57,560 --> 00:18:00,440
Let's try something else. Let's play a game.

297
00:18:00,650 --> 00:18:03,440
I love games.

298
00:18:00,650 --> 00:18:03,440
Shocking.

299
00:18:03,610 --> 00:18:07,400
Um, let's play the game of...

300
00:18:08,030 --> 00:18:10,320
...who can stay quiet the longest.

301
00:18:12,240 --> 00:18:13,740
Or Jenga.

302
00:18:14,950 --> 00:18:20,120
Let's play this one first, and remember, whoever talks first loses.

303
00:18:24,750 --> 00:18:29,260
I lose, now Jenga.

304
00:18:24,750 --> 00:18:29,260
Oh, my God. Oh, my God.

305
00:18:30,260 --> 00:18:33,510
Is something wrong?

306
00:18:30,260 --> 00:18:33,510
Wrong? You know the word "wrong"?

307
00:18:33,680 --> 00:18:36,270
Everything isn't perfect? Everything isn't magical?

308
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:40,270
Everything isn't aglow with the light of a million fairies?

309
00:18:40,520 --> 00:18:42,770
They were just brake lights, Parker.

310
00:18:42,940 --> 00:18:47,030
Well, excuse me for putting a good spin on a traffic jam.

311
00:18:47,780 --> 00:18:51,030
But you don't have to put a good spin on everything.

312
00:18:51,240 --> 00:18:54,280
I'm sorry. That's who I am. I'm a positive person.

313
00:18:54,450 --> 00:18:55,990
No, I'm a positive person.

314
00:18:56,240 --> 00:19:00,370
You are like Santa Claus on Prozac.

315
00:19:00,620 --> 00:19:04,250
At Disneyland, getting laid.

316
00:19:05,210 --> 00:19:07,800
So, what do you want me to do? You want me to be more negative?

317
00:19:07,960 --> 00:19:11,550
You want me to be less happy?

318
00:19:07,960 --> 00:19:11,550
Much less happy.

319
00:19:11,930 --> 00:19:15,890
Fine. Well, then to quote Ross, "I better be going."

320
00:19:17,810 --> 00:19:19,310
So long.

321
00:19:19,470 --> 00:19:24,480
Hey. Don't let the best door in the world hit you in the ass on your way out.

322
00:19:29,320 --> 00:19:33,950
Isn't this the most incredible fight you've ever had in your entire life?

323
00:19:36,620 --> 00:19:42,790
We could've gone from the ceremony to the reception with you in the sidecar.

324
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:44,830
Ross, it just wouldn't have been feasible.

325
00:19:45,080 --> 00:19:49,590
But having a dove place the ring on your finger would've been no problem.

326
00:19:52,130 --> 00:19:54,050
It was really fun being married to you tonight.

327
00:19:54,220 --> 00:19:57,350
Yeah, and it was the easiest 400 bucks I ever made.

328
00:19:58,930 --> 00:20:02,430
Okay, Ross, can I, um, ask you something?

329
00:19:58,930 --> 00:20:02,430
Yeah.

330
00:20:02,680 --> 00:20:07,310
That proposal at the planetarium?

331
00:20:02,680 --> 00:20:07,310
I know, I know. It was stupid.

332
00:20:07,520 --> 00:20:13,450
Are you kidding? With the lilies, and the song and the stars?

333
00:20:13,610 --> 00:20:18,700
It was really wonderful. Did you just make that up?

334
00:20:19,030 --> 00:20:22,160
Uh, no, actually, I thought about it when we were going out.

335
00:20:22,370 --> 00:20:27,130
That's how I imagined I, uh, would ask you to marry me.

336
00:20:28,040 --> 00:20:29,630
Wow.

337
00:20:30,550 --> 00:20:35,050
Well, that would've been, uh, very hard to say no to.

338
00:20:38,010 --> 00:20:39,680
Well, it's a good thing I didn't do it...

339
00:20:39,850 --> 00:20:43,480
...because it sounds like it would have been a very expensive wedding.

340
00:20:49,690 --> 00:20:53,400
Okay, good night.

341
00:20:49,690 --> 00:20:53,400
Good night.

342
00:20:55,900 --> 00:21:00,660
Uh, even if the sidecar had a windscreen so your hair wouldn't get messed up?

343
00:21:00,870 --> 00:21:04,200
I will think about it.

344
00:21:00,870 --> 00:21:04,200
That's all I'm asking.

345
00:21:07,920 --> 00:21:10,210
Okay, that's it. I give up.

346
00:21:10,420 --> 00:21:14,090
At Mom and Dad's 40th anniversary, you're the one giving the speech.

347
00:21:14,260 --> 00:21:18,010
You know, I don't understand why they didn't cry. It was a beautiful speech.

348
00:21:18,220 --> 00:21:21,300
Oh, come on.

349
00:21:18,220 --> 00:21:21,300
Hey. That stuff you said about true love?

350
00:21:21,600 --> 00:21:26,100
You were right. I mean, we did learn a lot from Mom and Dad.

351
00:21:26,310 --> 00:21:30,020
And that picture of Chi-Chi with her mischievous grin.

352
00:21:30,270 --> 00:21:31,860
And what you said about Nana?

353
00:21:32,020 --> 00:21:36,700
Oh, yeah, she really would've wanted to be there.

354
00:21:37,150 --> 00:21:39,610
And you know what?

355
00:21:40,700 --> 00:21:42,830
I think she was.

356
00:21:44,290 --> 00:21:47,960
Oh, good God, Ross, how the hell do you do it'?

